Fallowjack
Ingredients: TECH HOUSE + INDIE FOLK & FREAKFOLK + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Electric Guitar + 2010s ERA
Creative Catalyst: It was sparked by roaming desert camel-and-flatbed caravans who salvaged dusty Japanese synthesizers from abandoned solar installations and sold them to wandering acoustic troubadours.

Fallowjack is an unexpectedly buoyant collision of 2010s tech house club mechanics and the raw, communal spirit of indie freakfolk. Built upon crisp 126 BPM four-on-the-floor kicks, rubbery sub-bass lines, and skittering tech-house percussion, the genre bursts into wide-open joy with bright chicken-scratch electric guitar twang, eccentric gang shouts, wooden porch-stomps, and re-tuned, sun-scorched analog synthesizers.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 124–127 BPM; a driving, buoyant four-on-the-floor kick with crisp offbeat open hi-hats, layered over syncopated handclaps, clattering dry seed-pod shakers, and a bouncy, rolling tech-house sub-bassline.
- Harmonic Palette: Major Pentatonic and Mixolydian modes; bright, infectious campfire chord progressions (predominantly I–V–vi–IV and I–bVII–IV) voiced on jangling electric guitars and detuned, sand-worn analog oscillators.
- Production Signatures: Heavy sidechain compression pulling full-band room mics and jangly guitars into the kick drum’s breathing cycle; micro-sampled tactile sounds (finger-slides, boot heels on plywood, banjo thrums) quantised directly into modern tech-house percussion grids; saturated transistor overdrive applied to clean Fender Telecaster leads to match the bite of mono synth stabs.
- Dynamics: Starts as an intimate, quirky acoustic strum or muttered vocal riff, quickly swells through rising white noise and filter-swept percussion, and drops into an explosive, infectious tech groove layered with euphoric gang shouts and twangy guitar hooks.

Scene & Origin Lore
Fallowjack emerged between 2011 and 2014 along the sun-baked trade corridors between Tucson and the Salton Sea, where nomadic flatbed caravans scavenged obsolete electronic gear from decommissioned desert solar farms. When broke freakfolk collectives trading handmade zines and acoustic instruments bartered for salvaged Korg and Roland monosynths from caravan haulers, the music mutated overnight. Rather than playing slow ambient drones, they channeled early 2010s tech house stamina into rustic hoedowns powered by biofuel generators on dry lake beds. The subculture embraced a dusty, jubilant aesthetic: patched linen djellabas over denim overalls, neon surveying tape braided into straw hats, and boot soles reinforced with tire tread for all-night sand dancing. These "Dry Wash Chug" gatherings were marked by communal cider barrels, unpretentious joy, and dawn rituals where dancers shook handmade copper rattles in time with relentless four-on-the-floor rhythms.

Typical Instruments: Fender Telecaster, salvaged Roland SH-101 and Juno-60 synthesizers, kick-drum stomping boxes, five-string banjo, washboard, melodica, vintage spring reverbs, TR-808 percussion samples.

For Fans Of: Animal Collective (specifically Merriweather Post Pavilion era), Claude VonStroke, Tune-Yards, Folamour.

