Track 3: Who Marked the Dry Wash First?
- Story & Context: Years after the seasonal barter routes dissolved under newly erected border fencing, locals in the forgotten settlement of Ocotillo Wells swore the empty desert washes still hummed with a ghost kick drum every summer solstice. When county surveyors went to inspect the disputed fence line, they found only a web of sun-bleached patch cables, discarded horsehair fiddle bows, and boot prints permanently melted into the glassy caliche clay. Driven into the gravel was a hand-painted surveyor stake, left behind by the departed caravan and scribbled with their defiant parting anthem: "No surveyor draws a line that our stomping cannot cross."
